Functions are `@safe` by default

A function that is not marked `@safe`, `@trusted` or `@system`, and whose
attributes are not inferred, is now `@safe`:

---
void fill(int[] a) { a[] = 0; }          // @safe: checked
void bump(int* p) { p++; }               // error: pointer arithmetic in @safe code
@system void raw(int* p) { p++; }        // fine: explicitly @system
---

The first error in such a function is followed by a hint:

---
app.d(2): Error: pointer arithmetic is not allowed in a `@safe` function
app.d(2):        `app.bump` is `@safe` by default. If it must do this, mark it
                 `@system`, or move the unsafe part into a small `@trusted`
                 function with a safe interface
---

$(UL
$(LI $(B Still inferred, not defaulted:) templates, function literals,
`auto` functions, nested functions, and non-virtual member functions of types
declared inside a function body (safety only).)
$(LI $(B Still `@system`:) declarations without a body that use another
linkage (`extern(C)`, `extern(C++)`, `extern(Windows)`, ...), and all ImportC
code. Their code cannot be checked.)
$(LI Declarations without a body that use D linkage, including interface
methods, are `@safe`. An implementation must then be `@safe` or `@trusted`;
mark the declaration `@system` if implementations need unchecked code.)
$(LI The mangled name of a defaulted function includes `@safe` (`Nf`). Code
compiled by an older compiler does not link against it.)
$(LI `__traits(compiles, ...)` inside a function that is `@safe` only by
default ignores safety, so `static if (__traits(compiles, ...))` keeps
choosing the same branch. The chosen code is then checked as `@safe`.)
$(LI `__traits(getOverloads)` and `__traits(getVirtualMethods)` now return
functions whose attributes are known (including the `@safe` default).
Before, they could return a method's raw, unanalysed type.)
$(LI `@system:` and `@system { }` bring back upstream's default for what
follows: unannotated functions are `@system`, while templates and literals
still infer their safety and overrides still inherit it. They no longer apply
to variables; a variable is `@system` only when annotated directly.)
$(LI Generated documentation (`-D`) and `.di` headers (`-H`) do not spell
out the `@safe` default. A `.di` file read back by the compiler gets the same
default, so names still match.)
$(LI `-preview=safer` is deprecated and has no effect.)
)

Library changes (druntime and Fate). A `@trusted` function must be safe for
$(I every) argument, so functions that store or run a delegate they are given
now have two overloads: a `@trusted` one for `@safe` callbacks and a
`@system` one for any callback:

$(UL
$(LI `core.thread`: `Thread` and `Fiber` constructors, `Fiber.reset`,
`ThreadGroup.create`, `ThreadGroup.opApply`.)
$(LI `object`: `Throwable.opApply`, `ModuleInfo.opApply`,
`Throwable.TraceInfo.opApply`.)
$(LI `std.range.interfaces.InputRange.opApply` and
`std.concurrency.Generator.opApply`.)
)

---
void work() {}                      // @safe by default
@system void raw() {}

void main()
{
    auto t = new Thread(&work);     // fine
    // auto u = new Thread(&raw);   // error: needs @system or @trusted code
}
---

Now `@system` (they can corrupt memory with some arguments):
`Object.factory`, `TypeInfo_Class.create` (they run a constructor that may be
`@system`), the `std.experimental.allocator` interfaces (`IAllocator`,
`ISharedAllocator` and their implementations), `std.concurrency.Scheduler`
`start`/`spawn`, and low-level helpers such as `std.internal.digest`'s
assembly routines. `std.process.kill` is now `@trusted`.

Fixed along the way: `AscendingPageAllocator.owns` reported every address
past the start of its memory as owned.
